  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_section_1",
      "section_title": "Arterial blood gas (ABG) sampling syringe",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An arterial blood gas (ABG) sampling syringe is a specialized syringe used to collect arterial blood for analysis of oxygenation, ventilation and acid–base status."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Classification: Diagnostic specimen-collection equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Principle: A blood sample is collected from an artery and analyzed by a blood gas analyzer.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Key identifying features: A gas-tight syringe, usually pre-heparinized, with a compatible needle or closed sampling device and a secure cap.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Common sampling site: Radial artery at the wrist, when appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Main clinical uses: Assessment of arterial pH, carbon dioxide tension, oxygen tension and related parameters.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE distinction: An ABG syringe is designed to preserve the sample for blood gas analysis. An ordinary syringe may introduce air contamination or contain unsuitable additives."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "2. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing acid–base disturbances, including metabolic and respiratory acidosis or alkalosis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Evaluating oxygenation in selected critically ill patients.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing ventilation and carbon dioxide retention, especially in severe respiratory illness.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitoring selected patients with respiratory failure, shock or critical illness.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assessing response to respiratory support or treatment when repeat ABG testing is clinically indicated.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Measuring additional parameters such as lactate, electrolytes or haemoglobin derivatives when supported by the analyzer and the clinical protocol.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "ABG sampling is invasive. It should be performed when the result is likely to influence clinical assessment or management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Syringe barrel and plunger"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Collect and hold the arterial blood sample. Clear markings may help assess the collected volume."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Heparin anticoagulant"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Prevents clot formation. Many dedicated syringes contain dry, electrolyte-balanced heparin to minimize dilution and measurement interference."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Needle or sampling connector"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides access to the artery or connects to an appropriate arterial sampling port. Some systems incorporate needle-safety features."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Airtight cap or closure"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Seals the sample and limits contact with atmospheric air, helping preserve gas measurements."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "E. Sample label"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identifies the patient and supports recording of collection time and relevant clinical details."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "4. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides a suitable specimen for assessing arterial oxygenation, ventilation and acid–base balance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Gas-tight design helps limit air contamination.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pre-heparinized syringes reduce the risk of clotting.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Small sample volumes are often sufficient for modern analyzers.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Results can support rapid clinical decisions in emergencies and critical care.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "5. Disadvantages and limitations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Arterial sampling is painful and invasive.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Complications include bleeding, haematoma, arterial spasm and, rarely, vascular or nerve injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Air bubbles, clots, excessive liquid heparin or delays in analysis may distort results.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A sample that is venous rather than arterial can mislead interpretation, particularly for oxygenation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Results may be affected by the patient’s oxygen therapy, ventilator settings, temperature and clinical condition.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "An ABG provides a snapshot at the time of sampling and must be interpreted alongside the clinical picture.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "6. Preparation and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Before sampling:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm the indication, patient identity and requested investigations.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Explain the procedure and obtain consent where possible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Perform hand hygiene and wear appropriate personal protective equipment.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Identify a suitable arterial sampling site, commonly the radial artery.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check relevant bleeding risks, anticoagulant use, local infection and vascular-access considerations.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Prepare a dedicated, compatible ABG syringe and check its expiry and integrity.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Record the patient’s oxygen delivery device, oxygen concentration or flow, and relevant ventilator settings.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow local policy for assessment of collateral circulation when indicated; do not rely on a single bedside test as an absolute guarantee of safety.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Critical precautions"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use an appropriate aseptic technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove air contamination promptly according to the sampling-device instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not leave excess liquid heparin in the syringe; it can dilute the specimen and alter results.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Expel air safely, close the syringe securely and mix gently according to the manufacturer’s instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply firm pressure to the puncture site after needle removal, for an appropriate duration based on bleeding risk and local protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Treat all blood samples as potentially infectious and dispose of sharps immediately in an approved sharps container.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "7. How to use it — step-by-step OSCE demonstration",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a procedural overview for trained healthcare professionals following their institution’s sampling protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1: Confirm and prepare"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Verify the patient’s identity and request. Explain the procedure, obtain consent where possible, perform hand hygiene and prepare the equipment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2: Identify the sampling site"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Position the arm comfortably and identify the radial artery when suitable. Check the site and assess relevant vascular and bleeding risks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3: Collect the arterial sample"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Using the appropriate aseptic technique, a trained clinician obtains the sample with the dedicated syringe or approved sampling device, following local procedure guidance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4: Seal and inspect"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove visible air contamination promptly and safely according to the device instructions. Seal the syringe and check for clots or other obvious sample problems."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5: Apply pressure"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove the sampling needle safely and apply firm direct pressure until haemostasis is achieved. Use longer pressure and additional assessment when indicated by bleeding risk."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 6: Label and analyze promptly"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Label the sample correctly, document the collection time and relevant oxygen or ventilator settings, and transport or analyze it promptly according to laboratory policy."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_8",
              "group_title": "Sample handling: common sources of error",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Error",
                    "Potential effect"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Air bubbles left in the sample",
                      "May alter oxygen and carbon dioxide measurements and affect pH."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Excess liquid heparin",
                      "Can dilute the specimen and distort results."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Clotted sample",
                      "Can obstruct the analyzer or produce unreliable results."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Delayed analysis",
                      "Cellular metabolism may alter gas and acid–base measurements."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Incorrect patient or oxygen-setting information",
                      "Can lead to incorrect interpretation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Venous sample mislabeled as arterial",
                      "May lead to incorrect conclusions about arterial oxygenation."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "8. Contraindications and complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Arterial sampling may require an alternative site or a different approach when there is significant local infection, compromised circulation, a vascular-access concern or a bleeding risk that changes the procedure’s safety."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Recognition or prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Pain",
                      "Explain the procedure and use appropriate technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Haematoma or bleeding",
                      "Apply adequate direct pressure and reassess the site."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Arterial spasm",
                      "Minimize unnecessary manipulation and follow the approved technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Distal ischaemia",
                      "Assess circulation and seek urgent help if the hand becomes cold, pale, painful or poorly perfused."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Maintain aseptic technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nerve or vascular injury",
                      "Use appropriate anatomical technique and avoid repeated unnecessary attempts."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Needlestick injury",
                      "Use safety devices and dispose of sharps immediately."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Persistent bleeding, severe pain, numbness, altered hand colour or reduced distal perfusion requires prompt assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "9. Normal and abnormal findings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The syringe collects the specimen; the ABG analyzer produces the measurements. Typical adult reference ranges at sea level are shown below, but laboratory ranges and clinical interpretation vary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Parameter",
                    "Typical adult reference range",
                    "What it assesses"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "pH",
                      "7.35–7.45",
                      "Acidaemia or alkalaemia"
                    ],
                    [
                      "PaCO₂",
                      "35–45 mmHg",
                      "Ventilation and respiratory contribution to acid–base balance"
                    ],
                    [
                      "HCO₃⁻",
                      "22–26 mmol/L",
                      "Metabolic contribution to acid–base balance"
                    ],
                    [
                      "PaO₂",
                      "Approximately 80–100 mmHg in healthy adults breathing room air at sea level",
                      "Arterial oxygenation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Base excess",
                      "Approximately −2 to +2 mmol/L",
                      "Metabolic component of acid–base balance"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "PaO₂ depends on age, altitude, inspired oxygen concentration and clinical condition. Reference ranges for children and neonates differ from adult ranges."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_11",
              "group_title": "Basic ABG interpretation mnemonic — ROME",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "R — Respiratory: pH and PaCO₂ change in opposite directions in a primary respiratory disorder.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "O — Opposite: Respiratory acidosis has a low pH and high PaCO₂; respiratory alkalosis has a high pH and low PaCO₂.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "M — Metabolic: pH and HCO₃⁻ generally change in the same direction in a primary metabolic disorder.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "E — Equal: Metabolic acidosis has a low pH and low HCO₃⁻; metabolic alkalosis has a high pH and high HCO₃⁻.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "ROME is a memory aid, not a complete interpretation algorithm. Always assess compensation, mixed disorders and oxygenation separately."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "10. Pediatric considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use a dedicated syringe suitable for the required sample volume.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Minimize blood loss, particularly in neonates and small infants.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Consider whether a validated alternative, such as a venous or capillary blood gas, can answer the clinical question. These alternatives do not reliably replace arterial sampling for every assessment, particularly precise oxygenation assessment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use appropriate positioning, comfort measures and age-appropriate analgesia.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply pressure for an appropriate duration and assess distal circulation after sampling.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Interpret the results using age-appropriate reference ranges and in the context of oxygen support, ventilation and the child’s clinical condition.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "11. Mnemonic — GAS",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_13",
              "group_title": "G",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Gas-tight"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove air contamination and seal the syringe securely."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_14",
              "group_title": "A",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anticoagulated"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the correct dedicated heparinized syringe."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_15",
              "group_title": "S",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Send promptly"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Label accurately and analyze within the recommended timeframe."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "12. Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_blood_gas_abg_sampling_syringe_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is an ABG syringe used for?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It collects arterial blood for measurement of pH, PaCO₂, PaO₂ and other parameters supported by the analyzer."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Why is heparin used?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Heparin prevents clot formation in the sample. Dedicated dry, balanced heparin helps reduce dilution and electrolyte-related interference."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why must air bubbles be removed?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contact with atmospheric air can alter measured oxygen and carbon dioxide tensions and may affect pH, leading to misleading interpretation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why must the sample be analyzed promptly?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blood cells continue to consume oxygen and produce carbon dioxide and acids after collection, potentially changing the result over time."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the usual sampling site?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The radial artery at the wrist is commonly used because it is accessible and can often be compressed effectively after sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Name four complications of arterial sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain, haematoma, bleeding and arterial spasm. Infection, vascular injury and distal ischaemia are additional possible complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What information should accompany an ABG result?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Patient identification, collection time, oxygen delivery or inspired oxygen concentration, relevant ventilator settings and pertinent clinical information."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the difference between PaO₂ and SpO₂?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "PaO₂ is the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen measured from arterial blood. SpO₂ is the estimated percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen, usually measured by pulse oximetry."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
